INTERP["e1"] = """
### (e1) Does the sticky actuator increase the difference between the value estimates of Q-learning and SARSA?
**{'Yes, but only moderately, and mainly while the agents still explore.' if GP['modified']['all']['mean'] > GP['original']['all']['mean'] else 'No, not in our measurements.'}** We define the value gap as V_Q-learning - V_SARSA, where V is the average of max_a Q(s,a) over the fixed validation set (Fig. d.2, right).

For seed 84 the gap averaged over all {CFG.n_episodes} episodes is {G84['original']['mean_all']:.2f} in the original environment and {G84['modified']['mean_all']:.2f} in the modified one. In the first 20% of training (epsilon > {CFG.epsilon(int(0.2*CFG.n_episodes)):.2f}) it is {G84['original']['first20pct']:.2f} vs {G84['modified']['first20pct']:.2f}, and between 20% and 70% of training it is {G84['original']['middle']:.2f} vs {G84['modified']['middle']:.2f}.

Over five seeds the gap across the whole of training is {GP['original']['all']['mean']:.2f} +- {GP['original']['all']['std']:.2f} (original) vs {GP['modified']['all']['mean']:.2f} +- {GP['modified']['all']['std']:.2f} (modified). It is larger in the modified environment on {gap_larger_all} of {n_seeds} seeds, with a paired difference of {GP['modified_minus_original_all']['mean']:.2f} +- {GP['modified_minus_original_all']['std']:.2f}. In the high-exploration phase (first 20%) it is larger on {gap_larger_first} of {n_seeds} seeds ({GP['original']['first20pct']['mean']:.2f} vs {GP['modified']['first20pct']['mean']:.2f}). By the end of training, with epsilon = 0.01, the gap is small and noisy in both environments ({GP['original']['last5pct']['mean']:.2f} +- {GP['original']['last5pct']['std']:.2f} vs {GP['modified']['last5pct']['mean']:.2f} +- {GP['modified']['last5pct']['std']:.2f}), and there is no difference left to detect.

Both algorithms learn from the same kind of data: real sticky transitions, in which the next state already reflects whichever action was executed. They differ only in the TD target. Q-learning bootstraps from max_a' Q(s',a'), the value of selecting greedily from s' onward. SARSA bootstraps from Q(s',a') for the next action it actually selected, so its estimate includes the cost of its own exploration. The sticky actuator makes each cell's outcomes noisier: an attempted switch, exploratory ones included, is rejected with probability 0.2 (the previous action runs instead and the request is discarded), and whether that happens depends on the unobserved a_prev. Taking a max over noisier estimates gives a larger upward bias (maximisation bias), while SARSA's sampled target has no max, so the two estimates move further apart. At epsilon = 0.01 the two targets are close but not identical, because 1% of SARSA's next actions are still random, and the gap shrinks to the small values above in both environments.

As an extra check we ran the greedy policy from 200 validation states and compared the predicted values with the Monte-Carlo returns (bias = predicted - actual). In the original environment both algorithms are optimistic, with a bias of {CAL['qlearning_original']['bias']:.1f} for Q-learning and {CAL['sarsa_original']['bias']:.1f} for SARSA, which comes from state aggregation. In the modified environment Q-learning still overestimates by {CAL['qlearning_modified']['bias']:.1f}, while SARSA's prediction is almost unbiased ({CAL['sarsa_modified']['bias']:.1f}). For seed 84 the on-policy estimate is therefore the better calibrated one under sticky actuators. This check uses a single seed, so we treat it only as supporting evidence.
"""

INTERP["e2"] = """
### (e2) Why is the modified environment non-Markovian with respect to the observation, and how does this affect credit assignment?
An observation is Markov if P(o_t+1, r_t | o_t, a_t) does not depend on anything earlier. In the modified environment the executed action depends on a_prev, the action executed on the previous step, and a_prev is not part of the observation (position, velocity). The true state is (x, v, a_prev) while the agent sees only (x, v), so the same observation and the same selected action can lead to different next-state distributions. We measured this with a random policy over {NM['n_aprev_2']+NM['n_aprev_0']:,} steps in which the agent selected *push right*:

| hidden a_prev | P(push right is executed) | mean change in velocity |
|---|---|---|
| 2 (right) | {NM['p_exec_right_given_aprev_2']:.3f} | {NM['mean_dv_given_aprev_2']:.5f} |
| 0 (left) | {NM['p_exec_right_given_aprev_0']:.3f} | {NM['mean_dv_given_aprev_0']:.5f} |

The agent is therefore in a POMDP. The hidden variable comes from the agent's own past actions, so the transition probabilities it experiences from an observed state depend on its policy. That breaks the Markov assumption behind the convergence guarantees of tabular TD control.

Credit assignment suffers in three ways:
1. Credit goes to the wrong state-action pair. When a switch is rejected, the transition is produced by the previous action, yet the update goes to Q(s_t, a_t) for the selected action, which is also charged the push penalty. That pair is credited with an outcome it did not cause. The rejected request is discarded and has no later effect of its own.
2. Switching takes several decisions. A switch happens only if the agent keeps selecting the new action. If it does, the switch succeeds after a geometric number of attempts (mean 1/0.8 = 1.25 steps), so the result of one intended manoeuvre is spread over several transitions that one-step TD can only link by bootstrapping from aliased states.
3. Targets are noisier. Each discretized cell mixes visits with different a_prev, so every target averages over the hidden variable and learning slows down. The first success came at episode {TR['qlearning_modified']['first_success_episode']} for Q-learning in the modified environment against {TR['qlearning_original']['first_success_episode']} in the original, and at {TR['sarsa_modified']['first_success_episode']} vs {TR['sarsa_original']['first_success_episode']} for SARSA. Switching actions also becomes risky in itself, which favours policies that switch rarely.
"""

